{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when Pharaoh let the people go.</b> And afterward it says, ״פֶּן יִנָּחֵם הָעָם״ (Exodus 13:17), ״וַיַּסֵּב אֱלֹהִים אֶת הָעָם״ (Exodus 13:18), and afterward it says, ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 13:18). And one must wonder why He called them ״הָעָם״ three times, and the fourth time He called them ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ alongside ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים״, that is, weapons of war. And it would seem that inasmuch as they were בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל they had no need for weapons of war, and Pharaoh's sending them away was on account of their being בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל — and if so, the opposite should have been said." "And it appears possible to settle this in two ways. The first is, along the lines of what is brought in the Yalkut, ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ״ — חֲמֻשִׁים means nothing other than armed with five weapons. And a difficulty arises on this: is the warfare of Israel dependent on a multiplicity of weapons? For is it not written (Judges 5:8) ״מָגֵן אִם יֵרָאֶה וָרֹמַח בְּאַרְבָּעִים אֶלֶף בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל״? For HaShem, blessed be He, is a shield for them, and Torah and prayer are the weapons of Israel, as it says (Psalms 149:6) ״וְחֶרֶב פִּיפִיּוֹת בְּיָדָם״ — two-mouthed, for both of them depend on the mouth. And if so, what glory is this for Israel that they went up armed, as if their trust was not in HaShem, Heaven forbid? And even if we say that a person is obligated to act by way of nature to do all that he finds in his power to do, and what nature lacks the miracle will complete — nevertheless it remains difficult: why did Scripture tell us that each one had five weapons? And why do I need a count? And furthermore, if it happened by way of miracle or by coincidence that each one had five, no less and no more — is that not a trivial matter? And moreover, as it appears, Israel were not trained in warfare at all, for they were occupied with crushing labor all the days, and what need did they have for these weapons, for they had not been tested in them? Should they not rather have gone with staffs and sling-stones?" "Therefore it appears fitting to explain that Scripture comes to inform us that they had no weapon in their hand other than the five books of the Torah — divided into seven books, according to the one who says that the portion ״וַיְהִי בִּנְסֹעַ״ is a book by itself. And this is what it says, ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים״, that is, armed — all of it an allusion to Torah. And it used the expression ״חֲמֻשִׁים״ because the expression applies to the expression, and likewise ״מְזֻיָּנִים״, for the expressions of חֲמִישָׁה and זַיִן are both names of weapons of war, yet with Israel they allude also to the Torah. Or: ״חֲמֻשִׁים״ means the five books of the Torah as stated, and ״מְזֻיָּנִים״ means prayer, as it is written (Psalms 119:164) ״שֶׁבַע בַּיּוֹם הִלַּלְתִּיךָ״." "Now it is well known that Israel were not fit to receive the Torah until after they passed through the Sea of Reeds and after they journeyed in the wilderness, for at the Sea of Reeds they attained complete faith in HaShem and in the prophecy of Moses His servant, as it is written, ״וַיַּאֲמִינוּ בַּה׳ וּבְמֹשֶׁה עַבְדּוֹ״ (Exodus 14:31). And before they came to this faith, it is obvious that they were not fit to receive the Torah as long as they were in doubt concerning the prophecy of Moses and faith in HaShem. And after they came at the Sea of Reeds to this faith, from then on they were fit for the receiving of the Torah; yet they still needed to journey in the wilderness, in a land unsown, to acquire there the trait of being satisfied with little, for such is the way of Torah: ״פַּת בַּמֶּלַח תֹּאכַל״ etc. (Avot 6:4), and the Torah was given only to eaters of the manna (Mekhilta Beshalach [Vayisa] 2), for from the descent of the manna they learned the way of contentment that recipients of the Torah require. In addition to this, they became rooted in faith by means of the miracles performed for them in the wilderness, like the miracles of the Sea of Reeds, and into their hearts was given yet another instruction: that for two thousand years of chaos the world was desolate like the wilderness all the time it is without Torah — therefore they needed to receive the Torah to supply the world's lack. Now before Israel received the Torah, they were in the category of the rest of the nations and had not yet been distinguished by the perfection unique to them among the other nations; therefore they were not called by the name בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל, but only through the receiving of the Torah — then HaShem distinguished them from all the peoples to be unto Him a treasured people." "And with this the verses will be explained in this manner: ״וַיְהִי בְּשַׁלַּח פַּרְעֹה אֶת הָעָם״ — at the time of the sending they were still called by the name ״הָעָם״, for they had not yet been distinguished from the rest of the peoples of the earth, because they had not yet their weapons upon them — neither natural weapons of war, for they were not trained in warfare, nor the weapons of the Torah, for they had not yet received the Torah, and were not even prepared to receive it, for they had not yet taken root in faith. Therefore ״לֹא נָחָם אֱלֹהִים דֶּרֶךְ אֶרֶץ פְּלִשְׁתִּים אַף כִּי קָרוֹב הוּא, כִּי אָמַר אֱלֹהִים פֶּן יִנָּחֵם הָעָם בִּרְאֹתָם מִלְחָמָה״, because they were still in the category of the rest of the peoples of the earth; therefore they would inevitably return to Egypt, for since they had no weapon in their hand, obviously they would be defeated if they did not return. Therefore ״וַיַּסֵּב אֱלֹהִים אֶת הָעָם דֶּרֶךְ הַמִּדְבָּר יַם סוּף״, for through the miracles of the Sea and the wilderness they would acquire complete faith, and then they would be prepared to receive the Torah. And from that moment they would have their weapons upon them, and it would be as though they had already received the Torah, and they would then be called by the name ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, to distinguish them from the rest of the peoples of the lands. And this is what it says: ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — ״חֲמֻשִׁים״ means nothing other than armed with five weapons, that is, the five books of the Torah and seven books, or seven prayers as stated. And this accords with what we find (Pesikta Rabbati 34:6), that at the giving of the Torah the Holy One, blessed be He, girded them with weapons, etc. And see the Ramban, parashat Ki Tissa, on the verse ״וְעַתָּה הוֹרֵד עֶדְיְךָ״ (Exodus 33:5), whose Targum is ״אַעְדֵּי תִּקּוּן זֵינָךְ מִנָּךְ״ — for from there is a clear proof that the Torah is the weapon of Israel. And even though at the time of the ascent from Egypt they were not yet armed until they came to the wilderness, nevertheless Scripture says ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ״ etc., because immediately, as soon as they consented to go to the wilderness and did not say, 'How shall we go to the wilderness, not a place of sowing?' — immediately they acquired faith, and the beginning of acquisition is reckoned for them as though they were already rooted in it. Therefore it says ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ״, that is, at the time they went up from the land of Egypt to the wilderness, though the completion of the acquisition of faith was at the Sea of Reeds as stated." "A second way of settling these verses: the people mentioned here are the lesser ones of Israel and the mixed multitude, for they bear a single name. And when it says ״וַיְהִי בְּשַׁלַּח פַּרְעֹה אֶת הָעָם״, it speaks of the mixed multitude, for since he did not restrain them from leaving, it is as though he sent them off. For we find the language of sending used of one whom a person lets go, as in ״שַׁלְּחוּנִי לַאדֹנִי״ (Genesis 24:54), said of Eliezer the servant of Abraham, and as in ״וַיֹּאמֶר יַעֲקֹב אֶל לָבָן שַׁלְּחֵנִי״ (Genesis 30:25). So too the sending mentioned here concerns the mixed multitude, and for their sake He needed to divert them so that they would not return to Egypt when they saw war. But for the sake of the children of Israel He had no need for all this roundabout routing, for ״בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל עָלוּ חֲמֻשִׁים״ (Exodus 13:18) — armed, for they are children in whom faith is firm, children of believers who trust in the salvation of HaShem, and this is their weaponry. Therefore they were confident in battle. But every diminishment of trust came to them from the mixed multitude, ״כִּי קָרוֹב הוּא״ (Exodus 13:17) — that is to say: they are newly come from near at hand, and have not yet taken root in the faith."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:29.946424898Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}